# =============================================================================
#  backup.ps1  -  rescue user data before a PC is formatted
# =============================================================================
#
#  AUTHOR
#  ------
#  KUDADONBE (KD)  -  Computer Consultancy
#  kudadonbe@outlook.com
#
#  WHAT IT DOES
#  ------------
#  Takes the data off a PC that is about to be wiped and reinstalled:
#    - walks every user profile: the WHOLE profile minus the known junk, not
#      just Documents and Desktop
#    - skips OneDrive cloud-only files instead of hydrating them (that would
#      hang for hours) and lists them, so nobody thinks they were copied
#    - drops exact duplicates: one copy is kept, and every copy that was
#      skipped is logged together with the path of the copy that was kept
#    - keeps malware out of the new install: Defender scans the finished
#      backup when Defender is alive, and a sanitize ruleset quarantines
#      executables / autorun.inf / worm shortcuts either way
#    - writes TWO copies where it can: a partition that is not C:, and a USB
#      pen filled by priority (documents first, then pictures, then the rest)
#    - collects the rebuild extras: installed programs, printers, Wi-Fi keys,
#      mapped drives, network settings, asset number, BIOS serial
#
#  TWO WAYS TO RUN IT
#  ------------------
#    1. On the PC itself, as Administrator. The normal case.
#    2. With the disk slaved: pull it, dock it on a healthy PC, and give this
#       tool the path (e.g. F:\Users). Use this when the PC will not boot or
#       will not let you log in - and it is the SAFER of the two, because the
#       Defender doing the scanning is then one you can trust.
#
#  HOW TO RUN
#  ----------
#  From the pen or share menu, pick "backup". Or directly:
#     iex (gc <path>\backup.txt -Raw)
#
#  It is INTERACTIVE and takes no parameters (they cannot be passed through
#  iex, which is how the menu launches every tool). For a repeat or unattended
#  run these environment variables are used if set, and prompted for if not:
#     KD_BACKUP_SRC           source, e.g. 'F:\Users' for a slaved disk
#     KD_BACKUP_DEST          destination root, e.g. 'D:\'
#     KD_BACKUP_DRYRUN=1      plan it and write the lists, copy nothing
#     KD_BACKUP_NODEFENDER=1  force the sanitize-only path (for testing)
#
#  WHAT IT SAVES
#  -------------
#    <dest>\KD-Backup\<PC>_<date>\users\<profile>\...  the data
#    <dest>\KD-Backup\<PC>_<date>\extras\              rebuild information
#    <dest>\KD-Backup\<PC>_<date>\*.csv                what was kept, skipped,
#                                                      deduped, quarantined
#    <dest>\KD-Backup\<PC>_<date>\README-RESTORE.txt   how to put it back
#  The pen gets the same folder, as much of it as fits.
#
#  SAFETY
#  ------
#  It READS the PC and WRITES to the destination. It never deletes source
#  data, and it never lets Defender delete anything either - threats are
#  MOVED into _quarantine\ inside the backup, never removed.
#
#  The one exception is opt-in behind a typed YES: if there is no other
#  destination at all it offers to shrink C: for a rescue partition. That
#  changes the partition table. Read the warning it prints - a rescue
#  partition on the same disk does NOT survive a full-disk format.
# =============================================================================

$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'

